and to 200 thousand tokens. That explains the efficiency gain, but it does not give a Korean-specific ratio.\n\n**Other reported figures**\n- A blog on token counting gives a rough Korean estimate of 2.0 - 4.0 per character, which is a per-character range and not a ratio to English. Its single greeting example, not a systematic measurement, shows Korean at about 15 tokens against about 6 for English.\n- One post claims every Korean translation costs approximately 2.8x the English equivalent. It doesn't say which tokenizer it used, so I'd weigh it lower.\n- A tokenizer guide says o200k_base typically produces 10-15% fewer tokens for the same text compared to cl100k_base. That is a general claim, not one measured on Korean.\n\n**Bottom line:** For o200k_base, the best-supported Korean-to-English ratio from these results is roughly 1.3x to 1.4x, based on small samples. The cl100k_base penalty reported in the academic study was much larger, nearly 4x by character count. The gap between these figures likely reflects differences in method, text type, and whether the ratio is by tokens, characters, or sentences. If you need a precise number for your own workload, measure it directly with tiktoken on a sample of your actual text."],"durationSeconds":9.7108083,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01NXvka6fTZw4T3jrhCeMCRZ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PwdkVzQ92jU3hGucsrEBWk","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Claude tokenizer Korean tokens compared to English ratio\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"RedWhale: An Adapted Korean LLM Through Efficient Continual Pretraining\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2408.11294\"},{\"title\":\"DEV Community\",\"url\":\"https://dev.to/claudeguide/korean-vs-english-do-you-pay-more-tokens-for-the-same-prompt-jm8\"},{\"title\":\"which llm is cheapest for your language tokenizer costs measured 1k5m\",\"url\":\"https://dev.to/synthorai/which-llm-is-cheapest-for-your-language-tokenizer-costs-measured-1k5m\"},{\"title\":\"The Non-English Token Tax — Korean Costs 1.4x, Measured\",\"url\":\"https://jangwook.net/en/blog/en/multilingual-llm-token-tax-experiment/\"},{\"title\":\"26401 feature request non english users korean japanese cjk face structural disadvanta\",\"url\":\"https://claudeissues.com/issue/26401-feature-request-non-english-users-korean-japanese-cjk-face-structural-disadvanta\"},{\"title\":\"Korean Penalty of Large Language Models Derived by the Tokenizer ICICPE 2024\",\"url\":\"https://hiai.korea.ac.kr/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/Korean-Penalty-of-Large-Language-Models-Derived-by-the-Tokenizer_ICICPE_2024.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"tokenizer blindspots production llm\",\"url\":\"https://tianpan.co/blog/2026/04/20/tokenizer-blindspots-production-llm\"},{\"title\":\"the mystery of the claude 3 tokenizer\",\"url\":\"https://tokencontributions.substack.com/p/the-mystery-of-the-claude-3-tokenizer\"},{\"title\":\"[Revue de papier] How does a Language-Specific Tokenizer affect LLMs?\",\"url\":\"https://www.themoonlight.io/fr/review/how-does-a-language-specific-tokenizer-affect-llms\"}]\n\nI found no official Anthropic figure for the Korean-to-English token ratio. The available numbers come from third-party measurements, and they vary widely depending on method and sample.\n\n**Ratios from measurements**\n\n- **About 1.3x:** One blog post measured 8 meaning-matched Korean/English pairs with OpenAI's o200k_base tokenizer, not Claude's. It found a mean of 1.30x, with a range of 1.09x to 1.42x. Measured across 8 meaning-matched Korean/English pairs with the o200k_base BPE tokenizer, the mean ratio was 1.30x (median 1.33x, range 1.09x to 1.42x). The same post argues that the Korean/English ratio is roughly similar across tokenizers, though it offers no Claude-specific data to confirm that. It also notes that with older or less Korean-optimized tokenizers, the gap can be larger.\n- **About 1.4x, using Claude's tokenizer:** Another blog compared the same 285 articles across four languages. It used the Claude tokenizer from Hugging Face, which is a community port, not Anthropic's own release. Its title reports Korean at about 1.4x English, but the table with the Claude column was cut off in the results, so I could not confirm the figure from the page body.\n- **Much higher, per character:** A third post, which covers newer Claude models, reports that on Claude, Chinese runs about 114 net tokens per 100 characters, Korean 106, Japanese 94, against 32 for English. That is a per-character measure, so it is not directly comparable to a per-meaning ratio. The same post warns that read that column alone and CJK looks like a 3x tax, but it (the rest of that sentence was not in the results).\n- **1.5x to 3x, anecdotal:** A GitHub feature request claims that the same meaning expressed in Korean requires approximately 1.5x to 3x more tokens than English. Its examples are rough estimates rather than measurements, so I would weight this lowest.\n\n**Takeaway**\n\nKorean typically costs more tokens than English for the same meaning. Most measured estimates fall between about 1.3x and 1.4x, with higher figures appearing in per-character or anecdotal comparisons. I could not find a rigorous, Claude-specific benchmark. If you need an exact number, the most reliable approach is to count tokens on your own Korean and English text with Anthropic's token counting tools.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b70d8e57-a4cf-4267-9a92-5bd4cd15ea51","uuid":"5ddfaa9d-c7a2-4787-b201-c98bd27e4ac0","timestamp":"2026-10-18T07:48:05.345Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Claude tokenizer Korean tokens compared to English ratio","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_016FJ7Wx7nXEcPPEyNyxxfV8","content":[{"title":"RedWhale: An Adapted Korean LLM Through Efficient Continual Pretraining","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2408.11294"},{"title":"DEV Community","url":"https://dev.to/claudeguide/korean-vs-english-do-you-pay-more-tokens-for-the-same-prompt-jm8"},{"title":"which llm is cheapest for your language tokenizer costs measured 1k5m","url":"https://dev.to/synthorai/which-llm-is-cheapest-for-your-language-tokenizer-costs-measured-1k5m"},{"title":"The Non-English Token Tax — Korean Costs 1.4x, Measured","url":"https://jangwook.net/en/blog/en/multilingual-llm-token-tax-experiment/"},{"title":"26401 feature request non english users korean japanese cjk face structural disadvanta","url":"https://claudeissues.com/issue/26401-feature-request-non-english-users-korean-japanese-cjk-face-structural-disadvanta"},{"title":"Korean Penalty of Large Language Models Derived by the Tokenizer ICICPE 2024","url":"https://hiai.korea.ac.kr/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/Korean-Penalty-of-Large-Language-Models-Derived-by-the-Tokenizer_ICICPE_2024.pdf"},{"title":"tokenizer blindspots production llm","url":"https://tianpan.co/blog/2026/04/20/tokenizer-blindspots-production-llm"},{"title":"the mystery of the claude 3 tokenizer","url":"https://tokencontributions.substack.com/p/the-mystery-of-the-claude-3-tokenizer"},{"title":"[Revue de papier] How does a Language-Specific Tokenizer affect LLMs?","url":"https://www.themoonlight.io/fr/review/how-does-a-language-specific-tokenizer-affect-llms"}]},"I found no official Anthropic figure for the Korean-to-English token ratio. The available numbers come from third-party measurements, and they vary widely depending on method and sample.\n\n**Ratios from measurements**\n\n- **About 1.3x:** One blog post measured 8 meaning-matched Korean/English pairs with OpenAI's o200k_base tokenizer, not Claude's. It found a mean of 1.30x, with a range of 1.09x to 1.42x. Measured across 8 meaning-matched Korean/English pairs with the o200k_base BPE tokenizer, the mean ratio was 1.30x (median 1.33x, range 1.09x to 1.42x). The same post argues that the Korean/English ratio is roughly similar across tokenizers, though it offers no Claude-specific data to confirm that. It also notes that with older or less Korean-optimized tokenizers, the gap can be larger.\n- **About 1.4x, using Claude's tokenizer:** Another blog compared the same 285 articles across four languages. It used the Claude tokenizer from Hugging Face, which is a community port, not Anthropic's own release. Its title reports Korean at about 1.4x English, but the table with the Claude column was cut off in the results, so I could not confirm the figure from the page body.\n- **Much higher, per character:** A third post, which covers newer Claude models, reports that on Claude, Chinese runs about 114 net tokens per 100 characters, Korean 106, Japanese 94, against 32 for English. That is a per-character measure, so it is not directly comparable to a per-meaning ratio. The same post warns that read that column alone and CJK looks like a 3x tax, but it (the rest of that sentence was not in the results).\n- **1.5x to 3x, anecdotal:** A GitHub feature request claims that the same meaning expressed in Korean requires approximately 1.5x to 3x more tokens than English. Its examples are rough estimates rather than measurements, so I would weight this lowest.\n\n**Takeaway**\n\nKorean typically costs more tokens than English for the same meaning. Most measured estimates fall between about 1.3x and 1.4x, with higher figures appearing in per-character or anecdotal comparisons. I could not find a rigorous, Claude-specific benchmark. If you need an exact number, the most reliable approach is to count tokens on your own Korean and English text with Anthropic's token counting tools."],"durationSeconds":9.333130299999999,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01PwdkVzQ92jU3hGucsrEBWk","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
